{"records":[{"id":"mk7z7st438","title":"ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition. Rainey et al","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is required for activation of licensed origins of replication during the process of DNA duplication. CDC7 kinase inhibitors interfere with this process, limiting both DNA synthesis and cell proliferation.\n\nAim:\nIdentify genes responsible for limiting cell proliferation in response to inhibition of CDC7 using small molecule ATP-competitive inhibitors.\n\nStrategy:\nA CRISPR/Cas9 loss of function screen was performed to identify genes that upon loss allow bypass of a proliferation blockade imposed by the CDC7 inhibitor, XL413.\n\nData:\nRepresents source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the Manuscript 'ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition', Rainey et al.\n\nData collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-19T20:26:38.851Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mk7z7st438","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mk7z7st438"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Rainey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Replication","Cell Signaling","Genetic Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7rgn4p4cvp","title":"PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is crucial for the initiation of DNA replication and is important for replication stress responses. Several ATP-competitive CDC7 inhibitors (CDC7is) are being developed as possible cancer therapeutics. CDC7 inhibition causes a dose-dependent delay in S-phase progression and mild activation of the S-phase checkpoint pathway, which further limits origin firing through ATR activation.\n\nMethodology:\nTo identify genes that modulate the cellular responses to CDC7is, we established a CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide loss-of-function screen. The following data represent the closer investigation of one of these genes, PTBP1, using RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, immunofluorescence analysis and additional techniques as described in the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors'.\n\nData:\nData represent source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors', Göder et al. Data collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:30:16.52Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7rgn4p4cvp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7rgn4p4cvp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anja","last_name":"Göder","orcid":"0000-0001-9743-1656"},{"first_name":"Corrado","last_name":"Santocanale","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/501100001602","name":"Science Foundation Ireland","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","Cell Cycle","Cell Cycle Checkpoint","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nzrs6z3677","title":"Lung cancer cell derived exosomes enhance the metastasis of non-small cell lung cancer via SNHG12/miR-326/SLC7A11 axis","description":"The data are the raw data for the paper entitled \"Lung cancer cell derived exosomes enhance the metastasis of non-small cell lung cancer via SNHG12/miR-326/SLC7A11 axis\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-08T21:06:22.876Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nzrs6z3677","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nzrs6z3677"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rong","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Oncology","Experimental Oncology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mxrg37bftf","title":"Capillary associated macrophages regulate vascular permeability through VEGF / PI3K / Akt pathway to affect the effect of lung cancer chemotherapy","description":"Background : Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death worldwide. Chemotherapy is still the core treatment for advanced lung cancer, but the treatment failure caused by chemotherapy resistance is an urgent clinical problem to be solved. Insufficient delivery of chemotherapeutic drugs caused by abnormal vascular permeability in the tumor microenvironment is one of the key mechanisms of drug resistance. However, the regulatory effect of capillary-associated macrophages ( CAMs ) specifically localized around blood vessels on the vascular permeability and chemotherapeutic effect of lung cancer has not been elucidated. Methods : A mouse transplantation model of four lung cancer cell lines A549, H1975, H1299 and LLC was constructed, and a lung cancer cell-human umbilical vein endothelial cell microvascular co-culture system was established. Immunohistochemistry, Evans blue leakage assay, Western blot and RNA-seq were used to systematically detect CAMs infiltration density, vascular permeability and related molecular expression. Results : The abundance of CAMs infiltration in lung cancer tissues was positively correlated with tumor vascular permeability, and negatively correlated with chemotherapy response. Specific depletion of CAMs can reduce vascular permeability and significantly enhance the tumor inhibition of cisplatin without affecting microvessel density. Mechanism studies have shown that CAMs activate the VEGFR2 / PI3K / Akt signaling pathway in endothelial cells by secreting VEGF and TNFα, and induce VE-cadherin endocytosis to destroy endothelial tight junctions. Blocking this pathway can reverse the increase of vascular permeability and synergistically enhance the efficacy of chemotherapy. Conclusion : This study is the first to clarify that CAMs is a key regulator of the ' vascular permeability-chemotherapy drug delivery ' axis of lung cancer, and clarify its core molecular mechanism of regulating vascular permeability, which provides a new theoretical basis for targeting CAMs combined with chemotherapy to overcome chemotherapy resistance of lung cancer. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-26T11:55:37.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mxrg37bftf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mxrg37bftf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":" Jiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Cancer","Vascular Permeability"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t98pjcd7t6","title":"Single-cell lineage tracing of metastatic cancer reveals selection of hybrid EMT states","description":"The underpinnings of cancer metastasis remain poorly understood, in part due to a lack of tools for probing their emergence at high resolution. Here we present macsGESTALT, an inducible CRISPR-Cas9-based lineage recorder with highly efficient single-cell capture of both transcriptional and phylogenetic information. Applying macsGESTALT to a mouse model of metastatic pancreatic cancer, we recover ~380,000 CRISPR target sites and reconstruct dissemination of ~28,000 single cells across multiple metastatic sites. We find cells occupy a continuum of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) states. Metastatic potential peaks in rare, late-hybrid EMT states, which are aggressively selected from a predominately epithelial ancestral pool. The gene signatures of these late-hybrid EMT states are predictive of reduced survival in both human pancreatic and lung cancer patients, highlighting their relevance to clinical disease progression. Finally, we observe evidence for in vivo propagation of S100 family gene expression across clonally distinct metastatic subpopulations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-29T09:16:44.113Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t98pjcd7t6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t98pjcd7t6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamen","last_name":"Simeonov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Pancreatic Cancer","Metastasis","S100A Gene","Cell Lineage","Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"63y2gbj45w","title":"Biological Interpretation of Metabolic profiling fingerprinting of chemotherapy-resistant prostate cancer stem cells. A metabolomic approach by liquid chromatography–high resolution mass spectrometry","description":"Chemoresistance is one of the most important challenges in cancer therapy. The presence of cancer stem cells within the tumor may contribute to chemotherapy resistance since these cells express high levels of extrusion pumps and xenobiotic metabolizing enzymes that inactivate the therapeutic drug. Despite the recent advances in cancer cell metabolism adaptations, little is known about the metabolic adaptations of the cancer stem cells resistant to chemotherapy. In this study, we have undertaken a metabolomic analysis by western blot, quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qPCR), and fatty acid oxidation in a prostate cancer cell line resistant to the antiandrogen 2-hydroxiflutamide with features of cancer stem cells, compared to its parental androgen-sensitive cell line. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-29T17:53:49.427Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/63y2gbj45w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/63y2gbj45w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alicia ","last_name":"Bort","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Belen","last_name":"Sanchez Gomez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"León","orcid":null},{"first_name":"leonor","last_name":"CQAB","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose Maria","last_name":"Mora Rodriguez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florentina","last_name":"Castro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonio Luis","last_name":"Crego Navazo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ines","last_name":"Diaz-Laviada","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Drug Resistance in Cancer","Metabolomics","Metabolic Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3fjsc6y4n4","title":"Immunotherapy for EGFR Mutated Lung Cancer","description":"Patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) harboring epidermal growth factor receptor ‎‎(EGFR) mutations face poor outcomes after progression on tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs). ‎The efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) combined with chemotherapy in these ‎patients remains uncertain. ‎Therefore, we performed this meta-analysis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-08T16:34:10.35Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3fjsc6y4n4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3fjsc6y4n4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ezzeldin","last_name":"Ibrahim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Meta-Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvk2gk4hw3","title":"Data of Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","description":"Raw data for figures in Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-25T15:27:04.321Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvk2gk4hw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvk2gk4hw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139998,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":173,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139998,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139251,"restricted":69,"embargoed":678},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2320},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1418},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":674},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":454},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":332},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":276}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137345},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1909},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data